Allodynia (post-surgery / trauma related)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Pain from non-noxious stimuli, Hyperalgesia, Strict regional distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-004
Reason for Visit: Severe, unmanageable neuropathic pain crisis preventing sleep and basic comfort.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient in extreme distress experiencing severe pain triggered by simply wearing clothes or resting in bed following recent surgery. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute post-surgical complications (e.g., severe infection, compartment syndrome) and to initiate immediate neuropathic pain modulation to arrest acute physiological and psychological decompensation prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is on fire. Just the air moving in the room or the bedsheets touching my leg is agonizing."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a severe neuropathic pain crisis localized to the right lower leg, site of an orthopedic procedure 3 weeks ago. Timeline: Mild hypersensitivity developed 1 week post-op; escalated rapidly over the last 24 hours into severe burning and electric shock-like resting pain; culminated tonight in profound allodynia where the patient refuses to allow bedsheets to touch the skin. Sleep has been impossible for 36 hours.
Functional Status: Homebound, immobilized by fear of sensory provocation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute psychological distress and sleep deprivation compounding the pain experience.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Highly distressed, hyper-vigilant, actively shielding the right leg from any contact.
Physical Exam: Strict anatomical confinement of hypersensitivity to the L5/S1 dermatomes and the surgical region. Severe pain provoked by normally innocuous mechanical stimuli (light brushing with a cotton swab). Hyperalgesia demonstrated with exaggerated response to mild pinprick. Vasomotor instability present; right foot appears slightly mottled and feels cooler than the contralateral limb. Incision site clean, well-healed, with no erythema or purulent discharge.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is kept unseasonably cold as the patient states warmth exacerbates the burning sensation.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prescribed paracetamol and oxycodone, which patient states "does absolutely nothing for the burning."

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Neuropathic Pain Crisis with Allodynia - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Surgical Site Infection / Cellulitis - Ruled out due to absence of fever, no spreading erythema, normal tissue temperature at the incision, and lack of purulence.

Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS) - Unlikely as primary immediate diagnosis due to strictly confined dermatomal distribution rather than regional spread, though remains a long-term differential.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out due to the superficial cutaneous nature of the pain, negative Homan's sign, and lack of deep calf swelling/tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibited a classic, severe neuropathic pain presentation characterized by pronounced allodynia and hyperalgesia confined to a recent surgical dermatome. The profound nature of the pain—rendering standard opioid analgesics ineffective and preventing any sleep or physical contact—created an acute crisis state. Delaying intervention until regular clinic hours would have subjected the patient to an unsustainable level of suffering, severe sleep deprivation, and psychological trauma. Urgent after-hours assessment successfully excluded acute limb-threatening surgical complications, established the neuropathic origin of the crisis, and permitted the immediate initiation of targeted membrane-stabilizing pharmacology to interrupt the acute pain cycle.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Pregabalin 75mg STAT. Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg Nocté ongoing. Advised to cease standard opioids as they are ineffective for this specific pain phenotype.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Construct a physical "tent" over the leg using a blanket frame or pillows. Consequence: Elimination of unpredictable tactile stimulation from bedsheets, allowing for immediate psychological relief and sleep onset (negative reinforcement).
Action: Take the new neuropathic medication strictly 2 hours before bed. Consequence: Noticeable dampening of the burning sensation at night, securing a full block of restorative sleep (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for neuropathic medication titration and surgical team notification.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden onset of fever, pus leaking from the surgical wound, or a complete loss of motor function in the foot.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of neuropathic pain; long-term evaluation for conditions like CRPS may be required by a specialist. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Neuropathic pain requires specialized medications that take days to reach full efficacy. The sensitivity will gradually retreat, but pacing and strict medication adherence are required.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if the wound becomes red, hot, or swollen, if you develop a fever, or if you completely lose the ability to move your toes.

